WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.100
Memory constraints change secure C design because they make every implicit assumption more expensive.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:13.000
A desktop service might tolerate extra buffering, temporary copies, or generous log records.

3
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.740
A constrained C program often cannot.

4
00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:23.360
It may have a small stack, a fixed heap, limited static storage, narrow scratch buffers, and strict timing expectations.

5
00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.600
Those limits affect common security decisions.

6
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.340
Parsers need maximum input sizes.

7
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.040
Network and file handling need bounded buffering.

8
00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:36.340
Cryptographic operations may require temporary workspace.

9
00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:40.840
Logging and diagnostics consume memory that may be needed by normal operation.

10
00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:46.540
Error paths need enough reserved capacity to report a problem without making the system less stable.

11
00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:51.860
Allocation failure must be treated as an expected condition, not as a surprising event.

12
00:00:51.960 --> 00:01:03.080
If a design assumes memory is always available, the code may continue with incomplete state, skip cleanup, truncate output silently, or lose the information needed to make a safe decision.

13
00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:07.420
A constrained system needs clear behavior when memory runs low.

14
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.500
Stack growth is another quiet source of risk.

15
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:18.920
Deep call chains, recursion, large local arrays, formatting functions, and error handling paths can use more stack than the normal path suggests.

16
00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:24.860
The most important stack behavior may appear when the system is already handling unusual input or nested operations.

17
00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:28.340
Small memory budgets also affect sensitive data.

18
00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:39.560
Reused buffers, compact diagnostics, and shared scratch areas can leave keys, tokens, intermediate plaintext, or identifiers in places where later code does not expect them.

19
00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:45.900
The safer habit is to minimize copies, shorten lifetimes, and document ownership of sensitive buffers.

20
00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:49.940
The goal of this course is practical discipline.

21
00:01:50.040 --> 00:02:06.040
Secure memory-constrained C code defines limits early, validates lengths consistently, keeps allocation policy explicit, streams large input where possible, handles failure predictably, and verifies the real behavior on target-like builds instead of trusting estimates.
